/*!

\page cartesiangridplugin_python_interface_page Python interface

Everything the dialogs do can also be done from a Python script. The plug-in's Python code is in the module
<code>salome.CartesianGridPlugin.CartesianGridPluginBuilder</code>. This page explains the main functions; the complete
list, generated from the code, is in the
<a href="cartesiangridpluginpy_doc/namespaceCartesianGridPluginBuilder.html">CartesianGridPluginBuilder</a> reference
pages. A complete example script is given at the end of this page.

<h2>Creating the mesh and the grid</h2>

As with the other SMESH algorithms, you first create a mesh on a shape, then assign the algorithm to it:

\code
from salome.smesh import smeshBuilder
smesh = smeshBuilder.New()

mesh = smesh.Mesh(shape, "Mesh_1")   # shape: the whole geometry (the main shape)
algo = mesh.CartesianGrid()          # assigns the Cartesian Grid 3D algorithm
hyp = algo.Grid(nbCells=[40, 40, 20])   # creates the Cartesian Grid Parameters hypothesis
mesh.Evaluate()                      # optional: number of nodes and hexahedra, without computing
mesh.Compute()                       # builds the hexahedra
\endcode

<code>mesh.CartesianGrid()</code> returns the algorithm object (of class
CartesianGridPluginBuilder.CartesianGrid_3D_Algorithm). It must be called without a geometry argument, because the
algorithm must be assigned to the main shape.

<code>algo.Grid(...)</code> creates the hypothesis the first time it is called, and returns the same hypothesis on later
calls. Its arguments are all optional:
- <code>nbCells</code>: the number of cells along each axis (mode Nb cells);
- <code>step</code>: the cell size along each axis (mode Step);
- <code>coordinates</code>: the positions of the grid lines along each axis (mode Coordinates);
- <code>spacing</code>: a spacing function along each axis (mode Spacing). A value is either a function of t, as a
  string, or a pair (list of functions, list of internal points), as in Body Fitting;
- <code>keepOutsideCells</code>: <code>True</code> to keep the cells outside all solids.

<code>nbCells</code>, <code>step</code> and <code>spacing</code> accept either one value, used for the three axes, or a
list of three values for X, Y and Z. <code>coordinates</code> always takes a list of three. In a list, <code>None</code>
leaves that axis unchanged. Some examples:

\code
algo.Grid(nbCells=[40, 40, 20])                           # 40 x 40 x 20 cells
algo.Grid(step=2.5, keepOutsideCells=True)                # cells of size 2.5, keep the outside cells
algo.Grid(coordinates=[[0, 2, 5, 10], None, None])        # grid lines along X only
algo.Grid(spacing=[None, None, (["0.5", "2"], [0.5])])    # along Z: size 0.5, then 2 from the middle
\endcode

\warning Do not call <code>mesh.CartesianGrid()</code> a second time to change the grid: it creates a new algorithm
object with a new hypothesis, and that hypothesis is not used by the mesh. Keep the <code>hyp</code> object returned by
the first call, and change the grid with its methods below.

<h2>Methods of the hypothesis</h2>

In all these methods, <code>axis</code> is 0 for X, 1 for Y and 2 for Z.

<table>
<tr><th>Method</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>SetNbCells(axis, nb)</code></td><td>mode Nb cells, with nb cells (at least 1)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>SetStep(axis, step)</code></td><td>mode Step, with a cell size of about step (positive); the size is
adjusted so that the cells fill the extent exactly</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>SetCoordinates(axis, coords)</code></td><td>mode Coordinates: the positions of the grid lines, at least 2
different values</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>SetSpacing(axis, functions, internalPoints)</code></td><td>mode Spacing: one function of t per range;
the internal points, between 0 and 1, separate the ranges, so there is one point fewer than functions</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>GetMode(axis)</code></td><td>the mode of the axis: 0 Nb cells, 1 Step, 2 Coordinates, 3 Spacing</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>GetNbCells</code>, <code>GetStep</code>, <code>GetCoordinates</code>, <code>GetSpacing</code></td><td>read
back the values of each mode</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>SetKeepOutsideCells(keep)</code>, <code>GetKeepOutsideCells()</code></td><td>keep (<code>True</code>) or
remove (<code>False</code>) the cells outside all solids</td></tr>
</table>

For example, <code>hyp.SetStep(2, 5.0)</code> sets cells of about 5 along Z.

When a value is not valid, the method raises a <code>SALOME.SALOME_Exception</code> with one of these messages:
- "Axis must be 0 (X), 1 (Y) or 2 (Z)"
- "Number of cells must be at least 1"
- "Step must be positive"
- "At least 2 coordinates are needed", "Coordinates must be finite numbers", "Duplicate coordinates"
- "At least one spacing function is needed", "Number of internal points must be the number of functions minus one",
  "Internal points must be inside ]0, 1[", "Invalid spacing function"

After a compute, <code>mesh.GetComputeErrors()</code> lists the errors and warnings. Some examples:
- "Keep outside cells requires a compound main shape"
- "Solid #n is not closed"
- "More than 2147483646 cells along X: the step is too small"
- "Grid of a x b x c cells is too large to be stored"
- "n solid(s) own no cell, refine the grid"

The export raises the same messages, as a <code>SALOME.SALOME_Exception</code>.

<h2>Writing the Cartesian MED file</h2>

\code
summary = algo.ExportCartesianMED("grid.med", source="geometry", zonePrefix="MAT_")
print(summary)
\endcode

This writes the \ref cartesiangridplugin_export_page "Cartesian MED file" and returns a summary as English text: the
size of the grid, the number of cells in each zone, the cells in no zone, the solids in no zone group, and the warnings.
The mesh does not need to be computed first.
- <code>source="geometry"</code> (the default): each geometry group of the main shape whose name starts with
  <code>zonePrefix</code> becomes a zone.
- <code>source="solids"</code>: each solid becomes a zone, named as explained in
  \ref cartesiangridplugin_export_solids "One zone per solid". <code>zonePrefix</code> is then ignored.

It raises <code>ValueError</code> if <code>source</code> is neither of these, or if the mesh does not use the Cartesian
Grid 3D algorithm. It raises <code>SALOME.SALOME_Exception</code> if the hypothesis is missing, if the grid is too
large, if a solid is in two zone groups, or if the file cannot be written.

<h2>Other functions of the module</h2>

These functions are called on the module, for example
<code>CartesianGridPluginBuilder.SolidNames(mesh)</code>. Unless stated otherwise, <code>mesh</code> can be a
<code>smeshBuilder.Mesh</code> (the object returned by <code>smesh.Mesh</code>) or a CORBA <code>SMESH.SMESH_Mesh</code>.

<table>
<tr><th>Function</th><th>What it returns or does</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>ExportCartesianMED(mesh, fileName, source="geometry", zonePrefix="MAT_")</code></td>
<td>The same export as <code>algo.ExportCartesianMED</code>, starting from the mesh.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ExportCartesianMEDMessages(mesh, fileName, source, zonePrefix)</code></td>
<td>The same export, but returns the summary as a list of messages <code>(key, args)</code> instead of text. The wizard
uses it to translate the summary.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ReportText(messages)</code>, <code>REPORT_FORMATS</code></td>
<td><code>ReportText</code> turns a list of messages into English text. <code>REPORT_FORMATS</code> gives, for each
message key, its level ("info", "note" or "warning") and its English text.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>SolidNames(mesh)</code></td>
<td>The list of <code>(zone name, solid number)</code> used for one zone per solid, in the order of the export.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>CartesianGridMeshesOn(shape)</code></td>
<td>The list of <code>(name, mesh)</code> of the meshes of the study built on this shape (GEOM or SHAPER) with the
Cartesian Grid 3D algorithm.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>CartesianGridAlgorithm(mesh)</code></td>
<td>The Cartesian Grid 3D algorithm of the mesh (as a CORBA object); <code>ValueError</code> if there is none.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>CartesianGridParametersOf(mesh)</code></td>
<td>The Cartesian Grid Parameters hypothesis of the mesh, or <code>None</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>CreateGroupsOnGeometry(mesh)</code></td>
<td>Only for a <code>smeshBuilder.Mesh</code>. Creates one group of volumes per geometry group of the main shape. A
group is skipped if its name is already used by a mesh group; a group that stays empty (a group of faces or edges) is
removed. Returns the list of created names and the list of <code>(skipped name, reason)</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>X</code>, <code>Y</code>, <code>Z</code></td><td>The axis numbers 0, 1 and 2.</td></tr>
</table>

\anchor example_CartesianGridPlugin
<h2>Complete example: a block holding a sphere, one zone per material</h2>

This script builds a block of steel with a lead sphere inside it, creates one group per material, computes the mesh,
creates the mesh groups, and writes the Cartesian MED file.

\tui_script{cartesiangrid_demo.py}

*/
